  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "ARG-Avatar: Animation-Rendering Guidance for Efficient Feedforward Avatar Reconstruction",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22207",
      "description": "arXiv:2611.22207v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Reconstructing animatable 3D human avatars from images is important for applications such as gaming and augmented reality. Feedforward methods make avatar reconstruction accessible by reducing inference time to a few seconds, but recent approaches rely heavily on scaling model capacity, training data, and computation. We ask whether such scaling is necessary. To this end, we introduce ARG-Avatar, an efficient feedforward framework with only 68M trainable parameters that achieves SOTA reconstruction quality. Our key insight is that 3D animation and rendering already provide strong geometric correspondences that existing methods largely attempt to learn from data. In light of this, we design the Foreground Avatar Coordinate RoPE attention (FACRoPE) and Intermediate Token Rendering (ITR) pipeline to guide avatar tokens to attend to their corresponding patches. Experiments across multiple datasets show that ARG-Avatar consistently outperforms prior methods when using two or more input images. Compared with LHM++, ARG-Avatar uses approximately 9$\\times$ fewer trainable parameters, 4.8$\\times$ fewer training sequences, and 14$\\times$ less training compute, while running 6$\\times$ faster and achieving a 2.7 dB PSNR improvement on the out-of-distribution NeuMan benchmark.",
      "source_name": "arXiv Computer Vision",
      "published": "2026-11-18T05:00:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "LongCat-Video-Avatar 1.6 Technical Report",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18822",
      "description": "arXiv:2611.18822v2 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Recent advances in audio-driven avatar video generation have enabled increasingly realistic synthesis, yet achieving precise lip-audio synchronization, natural, human-like motion, and visual consistency over long sequences remains challenging. We present LongCat-Video-Avatar 1.6, which builds on LongCat-Video-Avatar 1.5 to improve expressive naturalness and reduce error accumulation during extended generation, e.g., exaggerated lip movements and overly red, temporally unstable lip coloration, enhance the naturalness of facial expressions and coordinated head and body movements. These improvements are driven by a refined post-training pipeline that integrates reinforcement learning, improved classifier-free guidance strategies for DMD-based few-step distillation, long-video rollouts, and on-policy distillation. Together, these refinements improve expressive realism and enable the generation of videos exceeding 10 minutes while maintaining visual quality and temporal consistency. Arena evaluations show that LongCat-Video-Avatar 1.6 outperforms its predecessor and competing commercial audio-driven avatar models, demonstrating the effectiveness of the improved post-training pipeline.",
      "source_name": "arXiv Computer Vision",
      "published": "2026-11-18T05:00:00"
    }
  ]
